---
/**
 * The response commitment, said out loud.
 *
 * Competitive review 2026-09-20, thirteen Metro Vancouver contractors read on
 * their own sites: every one of them sells "24/7 monitoring" and "automatic
 * dispatch", and NOT ONE publishes how many hours pass between the trigger
 * and the property being cleared. The best any of them promises is a reply to
 * a quote request within four business hours — which is a sales response, not
 * a service one.
 *
 * We already contract a response window in every seasonal agreement and run
 * tiered routes against it. Saying the numbers in public costs nothing and is
 * the one claim in this market nobody can match without changing how they
 * operate.
 *
 * THE TIMES HERE ARE THE ONES ALREADY PUBLISHED on /service-areas. Do not
 * invent a tighter number to make the section look better — this is the page
 * a property manager will hold us to.
 */
import { INSURANCE } from '../lib/site'

const tiers = [
  {
    window: 'Before 6:30am',
    name: 'Core routes',
    body: 'North Vancouver, Lynn Valley, Edgemont, Capilano and the Marine Drive corridor are cleared before the working day starts, on every snow day.',
  },
  {
    window: 'By 8am',
    name: 'North Shore full coverage',
    body: 'Deep Cove, Seymour, Blueridge, Dollarton and West Vancouver run on priority rotation behind the core routes.',
  },
  {
    window: 'Storm dispatch',
    name: 'Extended coverage',
    body: 'Downtown Vancouver, Burnaby, Richmond and Horseshoe Bay are scheduled and dispatched on the same weather triggers.',
  },
]
---

<section class="section bg-ink-950">
  <div class="container-site">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <p class="eyebrow !text-brand-400">Response commitment</p>
      <h2 class="h2 mt-2 !text-white">We put the hours in writing</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-body-dark">
        Most contractors promise monitoring. Monitoring is not clearing. Your seasonal
        agreement states the response window for your property, and crews are dispatched
        on weather triggers — nobody is waiting on a phone call at 5am.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-3">
      {tiers.map((tier) => (
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-6">
          <p class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-white">{tier.window}</p>
          <p class="mt-1 font-display text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-brand-400">
            {tier.name}
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body-dark">{tier.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    {/*
      THE GUARANTEE. Ben authorised this on 2026-09-21 after the conversion
      teardown: two competitors turned photo documentation into a named
      promise — Only Strata's "No Proof of Service? It's Free" and Pro Snow's
      "No record, no bill. It's that simple." — while we did more of the work
      and said none of it.

      Worded to EXACTLY what the system already does and nothing more: every
      visit writes timestamped photos into the job record automatically, so
      the promise is one we can always keep. Do not broaden this to cover
      service quality, timing or outcomes — it covers the RECORD only.
    */}
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-brand-400/30 bg-brand-400/10 p-6">
      <p class="font-display text-lg font-bold text-white">
        Photographed, or you don't pay for it
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body-dark">
        Every visit is photographed and timestamped into your service record automatically,
        not written up afterwards from memory. If we cannot show you the record for a visit,
        you are not charged for that visit. That record is also what protects you when a
        slip-and-fall claim arrives months later.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-3 text-sm text-body-dark">
      <span>{INSURANCE.short}.</span>
      <a href="/service-areas" class="font-semibold text-brand-400 hover:underline">
        See the full coverage map →
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
